raise <class 'TypeError'> paramsight: ``A.f`` was looked up on an instance of A, which is slotted (attrs / @dataclass(slots=True) / manual __slots__) and has no storage for ``__orig_class__``. The instance can't carry its parametrization, so the alias is unrecoverable -- silently falling back to the unparametrized class would yield ``NoDefault`` for the typevars, which is almost certainly a bug. Opt in with one of paramsight.slotted_strategies:

  from paramsight.slotted_strategies import (
      add_field, uses_class_swap, uses_side_table)

  # 1. Real ``__orig_class__`` field; survives attrs.evolve / copy / pickle.
  #    @add_field must sit *below* @define / @dataclass -- it runs
  #    first, so the field exists before they scan the body.
  @define
  @add_field
  class A[T]:
      ...

  # 2. Synthetic-subclass swap; no field pollution; survives
  #    evolve / copy / pickle. Trade-off: ``type(inst) is A``
  #    becomes False (``isinstance`` still works).
  @uses_class_swap
  @define
  class A[T]:
      ...

  # 3. Out-of-band table; no field pollution; does NOT survive
  #    evolve / copy / pickle; needs weakref-able instances.
  @uses_side_table
  @define
  class A[T]:
      ...

  #    ((2) and (3) are equivalent to ``_paramsight_slots =
  #    "class_swap" / "side_table"`` set in the class body.)

  # 4. If you only ever call this method class-side (e.g.
  #    ``A[int].f(...)``), no opt-in is needed --
  #    this error fires only at instance lookup time.

